\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Four-Panel Climatological and Morphodynamic Regime Characterization across Global Archetypes ($N = 175{,}392$ hours, 2012--2016).} \textbf  {(a)} Dimensionless Dean fall velocity parameter $\Omega = H_s / (w_s T_p)$ displaying Wright \& Short classification boundaries \cite  {wright1984morphodynamic} for reflective ($\Omega < 1.0$) and fully dissipative ($\Omega > 6.0$) states, showing the extreme Pacific swell tail at Torrey Pines ($\Omega > 20$). \textbf  {(b)} Deep-water wave steepness $s_0 = 2\pi H_s / (g T_p^2)$ evaluated against the critical threshold ($s_{0,\text  {crit}} = 0.025$), separating swell-dominated accretionary regimes ($98.0\%$ at Torrey Pines) from steep erosive storm seas ($28.8\%$ at USACE FRF Duck \cite  {birkemeier1985users}). \textbf  {(c)} Sallenger storm impact regime distributions (Swash, Collision, and Overwash) \cite  {sallenger2000storm} parameterized via dynamic Total Water Level ($\text  {TWL} = \eta _{\text  {tide}} + S_{\text  {surge}} + R_{2\%}$) confrontation with berm crests $z_{\text  {berm}}$ \cite  {stockdon2006empirical}. \textbf  {(d)} Scale-invariant dimensional collapse $\Upsilon ^* = \Upsilon / \Upsilon _{\text  {ref}}$, demonstrating that normalizing raw kinetic energy forcing by site-specific carrying capacity collapses multi-order-of-magnitude energy differences into a universal invariant distribution centered at $\Upsilon ^* \approx 1.0$.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
